The claim is a main effect of emotion on discrimination accuracy, with F around 114 and ηp² of .571 from roughly 85 participants. Emotion categories almost always differ in how easy they are to discriminate, so a replication should very likely find the effect again. The Greenhouse-Geisser-corrected df (3.3, 281.8) fit a design with about 5 to 6 emotion levels. Such a large effect is robust, and replications of this kind usually succeed.

The main risk is that the replication measures something slightly different or changes the design. I do not recognize the specific study.

