This is Bargh, Chen & Burrows (1996), Study 2, the elderly priming and walking speed study. The original had a small sample (N≈30) and a surprisingly large effect. The best-known replication attempt, Doyen et al. (2012), failed with objective timing, and it found an effect only when experimenters expected slow walking. Other replication efforts, such as the Many Labs-style and Pashler-type attempts, also largely failed. Some replications of Study 2 exist, for example Cesario et al. (2006) found moderated effects, but the overall pattern is poor.

The claim could appear in a replication database such as the Social Science Replication Project or the Replication Project. The likely outcome is failure.


