This looks like Bargh, Chen, & Burrows (1996), Study 2, the elderly priming and walking speed study. It had a small sample (about 30 participants) and t(28) = 2.86. Doyen et al. (2012) failed to replicate it with automated timing, and the result only appeared when experimenters were primed to expect slower walking. Many other replications of the effect have also failed. Some replications, such as the Many Labs-style or other registered efforts, may exist, but most evidence points to failure.

The claim here may come from a different paper that cites or reproduces the same design, since t(2S) is an OCR error for t(28). The text matches Bargh's Study 2, though. The replication in question is probably a Registered Replication or one of the Doyen-type replications, and the likely outcome is failure.

